NAME
    json - strictly parse and compact a JSON document with POSIX utilities

SYNOPSIS
    json [-c]
    json [-c] -- <file>

DESCRIPTION
    json reads one complete UTF-8 JSON document from standard input or from
    the selected file. A successful default invocation writes the equivalent
    compact JSON text followed by one newline. It removes insignificant
    whitespace but does not change string escape spellings, number lexemes,
    object member order or repeated member names. This is compaction, not
    semantic canonicalization, key sorting or numeric conversion.

    The parser implements the JSON grammar of RFC 8259 / ECMA-404, including
    top-level scalar values, arrays, objects, empty containers, escaped
    strings, and arbitrary-length decimal number lexemes. The grammar is
    checked through to end of input; trailing non-whitespace is an error.

    Actual UTF-8 bytes in strings are checked against the shortest-form
    Unicode scalar encoding rules; NUL and unescaped controls are invalid.
    Escaped UTF-16 surrogate code units (including unmatched surrogates) are
    admitted as the JSON ABNF permits, although unmatched surrogates are not
    interoperable Unicode text. A leading UTF-8 BOM is rejected. Repeated
    object member names are preserved, not resolved into a map.

    On any validation error, standard output stays empty; diagnostics identify
    the failure class and the number of input bytes already consumed.
    Temporary files are created in an exclusive
    directory and cleaned on normal exit or handled signals. The input is
    never interpreted as shell commands or evaluated as awk program text.

    This command is a standalone POSIX utility; it can be invoked directly
    without a preinitialized m environment.

OPTIONS
    -c
        Validate the entire JSON input and return a status; suppress output.

    --
        End options and introduce exactly one input file operand.
        Without --, input is always read from standard input.

OPERANDS
    <file>
        Read one existing, readable regular file. Use -- before the name.

ENVIRONMENT
    TMPDIR
        Optional temporary-directory parent; defaults to /tmp.
    LC_ALL
        Ignored for JSON parsing. The parser runs the POSIX utilities in
        the C locale for deterministic byte treatment.

DEPENDENCIES
    POSIX sh, od, awk, mkdir, rm, rmdir and cat.

EXIT STATUS
    0    Valid JSON and successful output (or successful check).
    1    Process interrupted by a handled signal.
    2    Invalid invocation or command option.
    3    File operand absent, unreadable or not a regular file.
    4    Invalid JSON lexical token or escape.
    5    Invalid UTF-8 within a JSON string.
    6    Invalid JSON structure or trailing data.
    7    Failed to create a private temporary directory.
    8    Error reading the input file.
    9    Error reading standard input.
    10   Internal byte-stream/parser state error.
    11   Error writing normalized output.

EXAMPLES
    printf '%s\n' '{ "a": [1, true, null] }' | json
        Output: {"a":[1,true,null]}

    json -c -- config.json
        Validate a complete JSON document without printing it.

SEE ALSO
    RFC 8259, ECMA-404 (second edition).
